BandC <onefunus@yahoo.com> writes:
> I don't totally understand the wishing concept.

The wishing spoiler already linked upthread is an excellent place to start.

> I only had to opportunity like twice I think.

Wishes are rare and powerful. They say, though, that in every game
there is a guaranteed source of multiple wishes deep within the
dungeon.

> Now you have to type the name of an item "exactly" right?

The parser is pretty sophisticated, but take care lest it end up
incorrectly guessing what it was you really wanted.

> Do you waste your wish if you type an unknown item or misspell?

If you wanted something, specify it incorrectly and end up with
something else, that probably qualifies as a waste.

If you stump the parser badly enough, you'll get a message along the
lines of "no such item exists in the game" and be able to try again on
the same wish. (You do not get an infinite number of chances, though.)

> If so, you have to know exactly what you are gonna wish. Isn't this
> kinda unfair for beginner players?

Players who are better at playing nethack are better at playing
nethack than players who aren't as good at playing nethack. I decline
to comment on the fairness of this or lack thereof, other than to
suggest that the universe at large and life in general are likewise
fair or unfair.

> I mean I don't know what to wish for. All I can think of is like:
> Iron skull cap +3.

If that is the most useful thing you've encountered while playing
nethack, then it is a perfectly reasonable thing for which to
wish. I'd suggest that a very modest amount of additional play will
reveal some better options.

If you're impatient, there's always the spoilers and/or
experimentation in explore mode. (If you launch the game in explore
mode via "nethack -X" or similar, you'll start with a wand of
wishing. Beware, as explore-mode wishing tends to work better than it
does in real life.)

> My point is: wishing heavily relies on past experiences of the
> player. I know many other features also rely on past experiences of
> the player but this one more so. Can you explain wishing a little
> and what do you all think?

There are at least two ways to interpret this question.

If you are objecting to the fact that nethack gives a new player
resources without first providing full information on optimal use of
those resources: This is an (apparently) intentional design decision.
That it is at odds with design decisions made in typical commercial
video games does not make it wrong.

If the problem is that nethack seeming requires you to "break
character" by using knowledge from outside your current game: Nethack
is not a role-playing game, despite sharing some of the same
trappings. You can certainly treat it as one if you so desire, but
recognize that this is a significant voluntary challenge over and
above the normal difficulty of the game.

